class:: PitchAmbiguity
summary:: Candidate entropy and octave competition
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Pitch
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/Pitch2, Classes/FundamentalCandidates, Classes/PitchConsensus

description::
Uses the same fixed candidate engine as link::Classes/FundamentalCandidates::. For K nonzero candidates with weights p, entropy is -sum(q * log(q)) / log(K), with q = p / sum(p). Fewer than two candidates gives zero entropy. Octave ambiguity is the strongest octave competitor weight divided by the leading weight, clipped to 0 through 1; competitors within 80 cents of a half, double, quarter, or quadruple frequency count. This is MIRa's descriptive statistic, not a new pitch estimator or calibrated error probability. The probabilistic candidate model is inspired by Mauch and Dixon (ICASSP 2014).

Like link::Classes/Pitch::, this family extracts control signals from sound; it exposes explicit evidence or contour information for synthesis control.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: in
Audio-rate mono signal. Use one analyzer per channel; mix deliberately before analysis when appropriate.

argument:: minFreq
Initialization-time lower frequency bound in Hz. Time-domain methods need at least two periods in frameSize; SWIPE benefits from eight periods.

argument:: maxFreq
Initialization-time upper frequency bound in Hz, greater than minFreq and below Nyquist.

argument:: frameSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. First result follows one full frame. SWIPE uses centred Hann windows from 256 samples through this maximum, aligned to the centre of the trailing frame.

argument:: hop
Literal frame fraction from 0.0625 through 1. Updates every round(frameSize * hop) samples; this is not seconds.

argument:: method
Candidate engine: \probYin, \yin, \mpm, \harmonic, or \swipe.

argument:: binsPerOctave
Literal integer from 12 through 96. Spectral candidate frequencies are minFreq * 2 ** (index / binsPerOctave); higher density costs CPU and initialization memory.

returns::
An Array: normalized candidate entropy, octave ambiguity, strongest octave-related competitor in Hz.

examples::
PitchAmbiguity computes candidate entropy and octave competition across competing fundamental frequencies. In this example, candidate entropy regulates the wet mix of a complex ring modulator: stable, unequivocal pitches remain pure and unmodulated, while ambiguous transitions and multiphonics inject aggressive ring modulation sidebands. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: tracks the real-time entropy metric.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("melody.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic melody fallback
            Saw.ar(Select.kr(LFNoise0.kr(2).range(0, 5), [220, 261.63, 293.66, 329.63, 392, 440])) * 0.2
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var result = PitchAmbiguity.kr(source, minFreq: 80, maxFreq: 1200, frameSize: 2048, hop: 0.25, method: \yin);
        var entropy = result[0].lag(0.04);
        var competitor = result[2].clip(40, 2000).lag(0.04);

        // Ring modulator driven by competitor frequency and scaled by entropy
        var ringMod = (source * SinOsc.ar(competitor * 1.5) * 2.0).tanh;
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + (ringMod * (entropy * 0.4));

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, result[0]) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("Candidate Entropy", ~miraBus, 0.0, 1.0);
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
